# Every repo in the org calls the reusable workflows in this directory, and they run with
# `id-token: write` (Infisical OIDC) plus pull-request and issue write. A change here executes
# with those privileges in ~28 repositories, so it is the highest-leverage path in the org.
#
# State before this file (checked 2026-09-18): the "protect main" ruleset already required a
# pull request and allowed no bypass actors, so direct pushes were never possible. What it did
# NOT require was anyone reading the change: `required_approving_review_count` is 0 and
# `require_code_owner_review` was false, so a PR touching these workflows could be self-merged
# unreviewed.
#
# This file is only advisory until the ruleset sets `require_code_owner_review: true`. With that
# flipped, a change to these workflows needs an approval from @ryaniosys/engineering.
#
# This is also the control that lets the callers keep `@main`: the shared workflow stays
# instantly fixable across the fleet, and the change itself is what gets reviewed. Pinning 28
# callers to a SHA would invert that, freezing each repo on whatever was current the day it was
# created and turning a security fix into 28 merges.

.github/workflows/   @ryaniosys/engineering
